% -s and a csv value carrying a newline cannot both be honoured: RFC 4180
% spells such a value across lines and sort(1) sorts lines, so the row below
% is torn in two and its halves sort separately.  Refusing the combination
% would cost every csv result that holds no newline, which is nearly all of
% them, so it is allowed and diagnosed instead -- once, on the first row it
% damages.  This case pins both the warning and the damage.
t(1, "nl\nhere").
t(2, plain).

t(N, V)?
